 of contemporary Ryukyuan linguistic practices and acknowledges the influence of Japanese while still maintaining a distinct Ryukyuan identity.  A purely native Ryukyuan translation would likely lose the specificity of the 'Digital Humanities' field, which is inherently tied to technological advancements and interdisciplinary approaches.  The transliteration approach, combined with the '-isutaa' suffix, provides the most accurate and culturally sensitive translation for academic use within the Ryukyuan scholarly community.  Furthermore, this translation aligns with the conventions of transliteration used in Ryukyuan linguistics, prioritizing phonetic accuracy and intelligibility for Ryukyuan speakers."
